{
  "image_name": "34d914f030eac703fa10f71300bce8f6.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how three biological materials—spider silk, wood, and arthropod exoskeleton—are built hierarchically from molecules into larger structural fibers. In each case, a specific polymer or protein sequence forms nanoscale building blocks, which then assemble into stronger, larger architectures.\n\nFor spider silk, a protein with terminal regions and a repetitive core appears to organize into small β-sheet crystallites embedded in amorphous regions. These nanoscale structures combine into silk nanofibrils and finally into the full spider silk fibre.\n\nFor wood, chains of poly(D-glucose) pack into cellulose nanocrystals, with alternating crystalline and amorphous regions. These assemble into cellulose nanofibrils, which are then arranged into layered cell walls, including primary and secondary wall layers.\n\nFor arthropod exoskeleton, poly(N-glucose), representing chitin, forms chitin nanocrystals associated with proteins. These build up into larger chitin-based bundles that are arranged in a helicoidal, twisted plywood-like structure.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes a common design principle across very different natural materials: simple molecular components are assembled stepwise into nanoscale crystals or fibrils, then into complex, layered or fibrous structures that likely underlie their mechanical performance.",
